  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    An annual observance held at the Frost Zenith — the sprawling refuge camp built into the skeletal aurora spires — marking the moment the Long-dark Edict's rationing broke against the first thaw. The name misleads. What it commemorates is not bounty but triage: ice yielding thin seals, krill returning in numbers sufficient to sustain those who had already gone without.
    
    The vigil is silent. Attendees gather at the spires when the aurora peaks and stand. Fire-cores supplied by the Nightshelf under Flipper Accord provisions provide the only warmth — a dependency neither culture acknowledges publicly. The dead from the winters the Edict governed still outnumber the living who attend.
    
    Thanksgiving's significance to the broader record lies in its role as catalyst: during one such vigil, a mystic performing deep-pressure communion with the ice triggered what would become **Rumzunkaidkak**, the pressure-binding technique now weaponized across multiple factions. The original contact reportedly surfaced every starvation-death preserved in the berg's memory simultaneously.
    
    The ice at the Frost Zenith weeps salt water at specific points during the observance — literal melt carrying emotional residue. Elders forbid drinking it. The younger generation finds this superstitious. The older penguins remember the night the aurora turned a particular green, and they chose to eat the memorial tokens rather than starve. They are not being superstitious.
